Subject: [PATCH] sampling: backend sampling stays on with a reasoning budget
 and with a grammar

With --backend-sampling the sampler chain runs on the device that holds the
output layer, and only what it leaves crosses to the host. Over the RPC
backend that is the difference between a few bytes and the scores of the
whole vocabulary per token (970 KiB for a 248K-token vocabulary).

A reasoning budget and a grammar are host samplers, and common_sampler_init
gave backend sampling up as soon as a request had either ("backend sampling
is not compatible with reasoning budget, disabling"). A chat request with a
thinking budget or with tools has one or both, so a server that serves
agents never sampled on the device. On a PS5 serving Qwen3.8-27B one stream
wrote 16.3 tokens a second that way against 18.8 without the budget.

Neither needs the scores for most tokens:

- The reasoning budget (common/reasoning-budget.cpp) counts tokens as they
  are accepted and changes scores only while it forces its end sequence:
  then every score but one becomes -inf. That is a few tokens of a reply,
  and only when the budget is reached.
- A grammar is checked after the chain has chosen: the chosen token alone is
  tested, and the scores are needed again only when it is rejected, to
  choose among the tokens the grammar allows. A lazy grammar (tool calls)
  tests nothing until its trigger was written.

So for such a request the device now keeps one job, the top-k. It runs a
chain of its own that is top-k with k + 1 (common_sampler_get_backend) and
returns those candidates; the request's chain stays on the host, whole, and
runs on the candidates. When the k + 1 scores all differ, the top-k of the
vocabulary is the top-k of the candidates, in the same order, and from
there on the host chain does what it does on the full scores, with the same
random numbers. The token is the one host sampling gives.

The full scores are fetched for the steps that need them, and those steps
run common_sampler_sample as it was:

- a token the reasoning budget forces,
- a token the grammar rejects (the resampling), and grammar-first sampling,
- two equal scores among the candidates, where the order, and at the edge
  the set, would be the sort's choice.

Fetching them after the fact is new in llama_context:
llama_set_logits_on_demand() marks a sequence, and
llama_get_logits_on_demand_ith() copies one row of the logits tensor from
the backend when the host asks. The tensor of the last ubatch stays where
it is until the next graph is computed; rows of earlier ubatches of the same
decode are copied before the next ubatch runs, so several slots in several
ubatches lose nothing.

It is used when the chain's first sampler that does anything is a top-k of
at most 1024 (no logit bias, penalties or DRY before it), without mirostat,
token probabilities or speculative decoding. Otherwise, and with
LLAMA_BACKEND_SAMPLING_CHECKS=0, the request is sampled on the host as
before, with the line it logged before. Requests with neither a budget nor
a grammar keep the whole chain on the device, as before.

The slot's timings gain a line that says how many tokens came from the
device's top-k and how many needed the full scores, by cause.

LLAMA_BACKEND_SAMPLING_CHECKS=verify is for testing: every token chosen from
the candidates is chosen again on the full scores of the same pass with a
copy of the chain, and the slot's timings say how many were compared and how
many differed. It fetches the full scores for every token.

Only the host changes: the worker runs the same top-k node and answers the
same tensor reads as before.
